comments.body:
So I think "those with a history of vulnerabilities" mostly speaks for itself but maybe we want to clarify to "those with a history of **significant** vulnerabilities"

My understanding in this case of "risky third party libraries" is a library that we think could be dangerous to our application in some way because they perform dangerous operations, they may not be well supported, they may have an unknown developer.

@elarlang does that definition help?

"Encapsulation" would be done to reduce the functionality within the library which is being used, not to protect ourselves against malicious code in the library functions we are using. It is more of a control thing so that we have greater visibility of which parts of the library we are using and therefore a better understanding of if we are passing through vulnerable code paths or not.

"Sandboxing" is where we want to protect ourselves from the library and in this case it would specifically require some additional security boundary, e.g. we run it in a separate microservice with minimal secrets or shared context.

@ImanSharaf does that clarification help?
